QTranslate for Debian
=====================

Build from the source tree with the command the user uses:

  DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS="parallel=$(nproc) nocheck nodoc" dpkg-buildpackage -rfakeroot -us -uc

There is no trailing ".": dpkg-buildpackage 1.21.22 on Astra Linux 1.8.7
rejects a "." argument ("unknown option or argument ."), and "." is not an
argument of dpkg-buildpackage.

The artifacts land in the parent directory:

  qtranslate_0.12.0_amd64.deb
  qtranslate-dbgsym_0.12.0_amd64.deb
  qtranslate_0.12.0.dsc
  qtranslate_0.12.0.tar.xz
  qtranslate_0.12.0_amd64.buildinfo
  qtranslate_0.12.0_amd64.changes

The debug symbols are in qtranslate-dbgsym, not in the main .deb.  The
dbgsym package is built but not published with the release.

DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS:

  nocheck        skip the test suite.
  nodoc          skip README.Debian.  The copyright and changelog are still
                 installed.
  parallel=N     pass N to the CMake/Ninja build.

Without nocheck, debian/rules runs the suite under xvfb-run.  The measured
45/45 run needed no trusted-client entries.  On a real Astra Linux 1.8.7
Parsec X server, however, the passive-grab X11 tests
(tst_hotkeymanager_x11 and tst_hotkey_selection_x11) can be denied until
their build/debian/tests/... paths are in the trusted-client list:

  /etc/X11/trusted.d/qtranslate
    /path/to/qtranslate/build/debian/tests/tst_hotkeymanager_x11(KBD_R)
    /path/to/qtranslate/build/debian/tests/tst_hotkey_selection_x11(KBD_R)

  sudo /usr/bin/fly-set-execid.sh

This is the fallback when Xvfb or the running X server denies the grabs; the
nocheck build never needs it.

Source package
--------------

debian/source/format is "3.0 (native)": the source tarball is built from the
current tree, not from a VCS export, and the version in debian/changelog
carries no Debian revision.

debian/source/options keeps the local CMake build directories and the local
tool state out of qtranslate_0.12.0.tar.xz, even when the tree is copied with
its .git directory and a populated build/:

  tar-ignore              the bare line restores dpkg-source's default
                          exclusions (.git, .gitignore, *.o, *.so, *.a, *.la,
                          .svn, ...); a tar-ignore line with a value replaces
                          that default list instead of extending it
  tar-ignore = build
  tar-ignore = build-*
  tar-ignore = out
  tar-ignore = .claude

dpkg-source -b <dir> writes the .dsc and the .tar.xz into the current
directory, and it has to run on a cleaned tree: the exclusions above do not
cover the debhelper residue (debian/qtranslate/, debian/qtranslate.substvars,
debian/debhelper-build-stamp), so run debian/rules clean first, as
dpkg-buildpackage does.  dpkg-source always excludes debian/files itself.
dpkg-source -x qtranslate_0.12.0.dsc extracts qtranslate-0.12.0/ without the
VCS metadata.
